%[text] %[text:anchor:T_3AA44D56] # Fourier Series
%[text] [⇦ Return to the main menu](file:../MainMenu.m)
%[text] When a guitar is strummed, the vibration of the strings and guitar body emits a pressure wave. The amplitude of a recorded guitar note is plotted here in the time domain. If you wanted to determine the signal's fundamental frequency to use in a guitar tuner, how would you do it? A cursory examination of the signal in the time domain reveals little. Zooming in on the signal reveals a series of repeating patterns. Even from this view, it's challenging to determine which frequencies are present. There's an easier way to analyze this signal: plot it in the frequency domain.
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![guitar And Signal](text:image:2f73)
%%
%[text:tableOfContents]{"heading":"**Table of Contents**"}
%%
