%[text] %[text:anchor:H_C90FF8C0] ## The Frequency Domain
%[text] Fourier analysis is all about taking signals from the time domain (or spatial domain) and analyzing them in the *frequency domain*. 
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![timedomain](text:image:242c) ![freqDomain](text:image:4a4c)
%[text]{"align":"center"} *A signal in the time and frequency domains. While the signal has a lot going on in the time domain, it is actually a simple sum of two sine functions, as illustrated in the frequency domain.*
%[text] Each frequency present in a signal creates a peak in the frequency domain. The stronger that frequency is in the signal, the larger the peak is in the frequency domain. 
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_F7926997] ### A Single Sine Function
%[text] Consider the function 
%[text]{"align":"center"} $y(t) = A \\sin( 2\\pi f t)${"altText":"y(t) = A \sin( 2\pi f t)"}
%[text] This sine function has a frequency of $f${"altText":"f"} Hz. Suppose that $f = 220${"altText":"f = 220"} Hz. When represented in the time domain, the function is plotted against time, producing the usual sine curve. In the frequency domain, the function is represented by a single peak at 220 Hz. The amplitude $A${"altText":"A"} determines the height of the peak in the frequency domain. The plots below illustrate $y${"altText":"y"} in both the time and frequency domains. 
%[text] **Task.** Run this section to generate the plots. Click the blue bar on the left
%[text]  ![](text:image:9ca0) 
%[text] or click ![](text:image:2c9a) **Run Section** from the **Section** section of the **Live Editor** tab.
%[text] **Task.** Try adjusting the sliders to change the curve.
%[text] - What happens in each plot when you increase $f${"altText":"f"}, the sine's frequency?
%[text] - How do the plots change when the amplitude $A${"altText":"A"} is increased? \
f = 220; %[control:slider:2d1e]{"position":[5,8]}
A = 1.5; %[control:slider:8ade]{"position":[5,8]}
plotSingleSine(A,f); % Helper function that generates the plots
%%
