%[text] %[text:anchor:H_089AF9E7] ## Fourier Sine and Cosine Series
%[text] To accurately reproduce a periodic real-world signal, it is often the case that more than three frequencies are required. As more frequencies are used, writing the function with many sine terms becomes increasingly cumbersome. Instead, you can use *summation notation.* For example, you could rewrite the function $y(t)${"altText":"y(t)"} from the previous example as
%[text]{"align":"center"} $y(t) = \\sum\_{i=1}^3 b\_i \\sin( 2\\pi f\_i t) ${"altText":"y(t) = \sum\_{i=1}^3 b\_i \sin( 2\pi f\_i t) "}
%[text] Fourier series reproduce **periodic** signals using infinite frequencies (in theory). In practical signal processing, only a finite number of frequencies are used. The *Fourier sine series* is an infinite sum of sine functions:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $f(t) = \\sum\_{n=1}^\\infty b\_n \\sin \\left( \\frac{ n \\pi } {L}  t \\right)${"altText":"f(t) = \sum\_{n=1}^\infty b\_n \sin \left( \frac{ n \pi } {L}  t \right)"}
%[text] $L${"altText":"L"} is a parameter that determines the periodicity of the function $f${"altText":"f"}, which has period $2L${"altText":"2L"}. This implies that $f${"altText":"f"} will always have periodic copies every $2L${"altText":"2L"}. Each term in the sum is known as a *Fourier* *mode*. For example, the fundamental mode (with $n = 1${"altText":"n = 1"}) is
%[text]{"align":"center"} $f\_1 = \\sin \\left( \\frac{\\pi}{L} t \\right)${"altText":"f\_1 = \sin \left( \frac{\pi}{L} t \right)"} 
%[text] The first coefficient $b\_1${"altText":"b\_1"} determines how strongly this mode is represented in the overall sum.
%[text]  ![interact](text:image:0537) **Activity.** In this activity, you will build several functions using the Sine and Cosine Series app. Click the link to open the app, then complete the tasks listed below.
%[text]{"align":"center"} [![SinCosSeriesApp](text:image:7a4c)](<matlab: SinCosSeries>)
%[text]{"align":"center"} [SinCosSeries.mlapp](<matlab: SinCosSeries>)
%[text] **Task 1.** Set the Fourier basis to **sine**.
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![basis](text:image:0721)
