%[text] %[text:anchor:H_83A00657] ## Fourier Series
%[text] The Fourier sine series contains infinitely many frequencies. This allows the series to produce many different waveforms. However, all frequencies produced using only sine modes will have zero *phase*. 
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![phase](text:image:1caa)
%[text]{"align":"center"} *The phase or phase shift of a signal is how far it has been shifted horizontally.* 
%[text] Real signals do not typically have zero phase, so the Fourier sine series is insufficient to represent them. However, adding a cosine series allows you to produce signals with any phase. 
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_9919791B] ### **Definition**
%[text] One standard definition for the Fourier series is the sum of cosine and sine series:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $f(t) = \\frac{a\_0}{2}  + \\sum\_{n=1}^\\infty a\_n \\cos\\left( \\frac{ n \\pi } {L}  t \\right) + b\_n \\sin \\left( \\frac{ n \\pi } {L}  t \\right)${"altText":"f(t) = \frac{a\_0}{2}  + \sum\_{n=1}^\infty a\_n \cos\left( \frac{ n \pi } {L}  t \right) + b\_n \sin \left( \frac{ n \pi } {L}  t \right)"}
%[text] With this addition, there are now two sets of coefficients: the cosine series coefficients $a\_n${"altText":"a\_n"} and the sine coefficients $b\_n${"altText":"b\_n"}. The sine terms have a phase of $0^\\circ${"altText":"0^\circ"} while the cosine signals have a phase of $90^\\circ${"altText":"90^\circ"} (assuming that zero phase corresponds to a sine signal). If you combine a cosine and sine signal with the same frequency, it produces a phase-shifted signal:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $a \\cos (\\omega t) + b \\sin( \\omega t) = c \\sin( \\omega t  -  \\phi)${"altText":"a \cos (\omega t) + b \sin( \omega t) = c \sin( \omega t  -  \phi)"}
%[text] where the ratio of the coefficients $a${"altText":"a"} and $b${"altText":"b"} determines the phase shift, $\\phi${"altText":"\phi"}.
%[text]  ![interact](text:image:1fdf) **Activity.** Create phase-shifted signals using Fourier series by completing the tasks below.
%[text] **Task 1.** Open the Interactive Fourier Series app.
%[text]{"align":"center"} [![FourierSeriesApp](text:image:1b8f)](<matlab: InteractiveFourierSeries>)
%[text]{"align":"center"} [InteractiveFourierSeries.mlapp](<matlab: InteractiveFourierSeries>)
%[text] In the app, $L = 1/2${"altText":"L = 1\/2"} by default, so the period of the Fourier series is 1 s. This implies that the fundamental mode with index $n = 1${"altText":"n = 1"} has a frequency of 1 Hz. To adjust the mode coefficients, click and drag the yellow circles. For this exercise, do not drag the blue circles.
%[text] **Task 2.** Adjust the Fourier coefficients to create a 1 Hz signal with zero phase ($\\phi = 0 ^\\circ${"altText":"\phi = 0 ^\circ"}) (relative to a sine function).
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![0phase](text:image:975c)
%[text] **Task 3.** Adjust the Fourier coefficients to create a 2 Hz signal with a phase shift of $\\phi = 90 ^\\circ${"altText":"\phi = 90 ^\circ"} (relative to a sine function).
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![0phaseCosine](text:image:7848)
%[text] **Task 4.** A 1 Hz signal with a phase shift of $\\phi = 45^\\circ${"altText":"\phi = 45^\circ"} (relative to a sine function).
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![45phase](text:image:204e)
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_BF75E823] ## Solving for Fourier Series Coefficients
%[text] The guitar A note audio signal clearly has at least three frequencies.
%[text] **Task.** Run this section to show the guitar signal.
% This code plays the guitar sound and plots part of the signal
[yGuitar,FsGuitar] = audioread("Anote.mp3");
soundsc(yGuitar,FsGuitar)
plotGuitarSignal(yGuitar,FsGuitar,[0.3 0.33])
  %[control:button:4f7f]{"position":[1,2]}
%[text] But what are the frequencies, and how strongly is each represented? In other words, what does the guitar audio signal look like in the frequency domain?
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_13B3CA5C] ### Fourier Coefficient Formulas
%[text] In the previous activities, you adjusted the Fourier coefficients by hand. Fortunately, this isn't necessary to identify the Fourier series coefficients of a given signal. You can identify the coefficients using two formulas:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $a\_n = \\frac{1}{L} \\int\_{-L}^{L} y(t) \\cos \\left( \\frac{ n \\pi }{L} t \\right) dt${"altText":"a\_n = \frac{1}{L} \int\_{-L}^{L} y(t) \cos \left( \frac{ n \pi }{L} t \right) dt"}
%[text]{"align":"center"} $b\_n = \\frac{1}{L} \\int\_{-L}^{L} y(t) \\sin\\left( \\frac{ n \\pi }{L} t \\right) dt${"altText":"b\_n = \frac{1}{L} \int\_{-L}^{L} y(t) \sin\left( \frac{ n \pi }{L} t \right) dt"}
%[text] These integrals calculate the weight of the $n^{th}${"altText":"n^{th}"} mode required to match the given signal, $y(t)${"altText":"y(t)"}. Each mode has a unique frequency, so these integrals determine how strongly each frequency is represented in the original signal $y(t)${"altText":"y(t)"}. Note that the function $y(t)${"altText":"y(t)"} must be periodic with a period of $2L${"altText":"2L"} to be represented by the Fourier series. If it is not, then the derived Fourier series will not match $y(t)${"altText":"y(t)"} everywhere, but will create a periodic extension of the original function with period $2L${"altText":"2L"}.
%%
%[text] The plot below shows the Fourier modes of the guitar signal estimated using the integral formulas and the trapezoid rule.
%[text] **Task.** Run the section to see the guitar A note in the frequency domain.
% Helper functions that estimate and plot the Fourier coefficients
[freq,a,b] = computeGuitarFourierCoef(yGuitar,FsGuitar);
plotGuitarCoef(freq,a,b)
  %[control:button:72bf]{"position":[1,2]}
%[text] Try clicking on a peak to create a data tip. You can use this to identify the frequency of a mode.
%[text] ![brightbulb](text:image:1e84) **Reflect.** 
%[text] - Which frequency is dominant?
%[text] - Are the dominant frequencies purely sine or cosine? 
%[text] - Based on the observed coefficients, can you make any general statements about the phase of each mode? \
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_375B350C] ## **Magnitude and Phase Fourier Series**
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_664C5632] ### **Magnitude**
%[text] From the frequency domain plot above, it may appear that the 330 Hz signal has nearly equal amplitude to the 220 Hz signal. However, this is not the case because the magnitude of a frequency is a combination of both coefficients. Specifically, the magnitude of the $n^{th}${"altText":"n^{th}"} mode is
%[text]{"align":"center"} $A\_n = \\sqrt{ {a\_n}^2 + {b\_n}^2 }${"altText":"A\_n = \sqrt{ {a\_n}^2 + {b\_n}^2 }"}
%[text] ![write](text:image:3a21) **Exercise.**
%[text] **Task 1.** Ensure that the previous section was run to produce the variables: `freq`, `a`, and `b`. 
%[text] **Task 2.** Compute the magnitude of the coefficients ($A\_n${"altText":"A\_n"}) from the vectors `a` and `b`. Store the result in `A`. 
%[text] ![matlab](text:image:1f93) **Pro-tip.** Perform element-wise operations on vectors by prepending a period character. For example, to compute the square of the coefficient vector `a`, use `a.^2`.
% Compute A here
% A should be a vector that contains the magnitudes

%[text] **Task 3.** Plot the magnitudes in `A` against the frequencies in `freq` using a [`stem`](https://www.mathworks.com/help/matlab/ref/stem.html) plot. Set the frequency limits to \[0,600\] using the [`xlim`](https://www.mathworks.com/help/matlab/ref/xlim.html) function.


%[text] ![brightbulb](text:image:5b78) **Reflect.** 
%[text] - Which frequency is dominant?
%[text] - The fundamental frequency of the guitar A note is 110 Hz. Does this plot make sense for the guitar A note? \
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_4E494A73] ### **Magnitude and Phase Plots**
%[text] The frequency domain can be visualized by plotting the coefficients $a\_n${"altText":"a\_n"}  and $b\_n${"altText":"b\_n"} or by plotting the magnitude $A\_n${"altText":"A\_n"}. The magnitude by itself doesn't tell the full story, however. A more complete picture can be obtained by adding a plot of the phase.
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![5modesSignal](text:image:6113)![5modesMagPhase](text:image:2ff1)
%[text]{"align":"center"} *The 5 main modes of the guitar A note* *signal in the time domain and frequency domain.* 
%[text]{"align":"center"} *Here, the frequency domain is plotted using magnitude and phase.*
%[text] This plot is often more useful than the plot of $a\_n${"altText":"a\_n"} and $b\_n${"altText":"b\_n"} because it clearly illustrates two intuitive features of each mode:
%[text] - **magnitude**: the strength of the mode
%[text] - **phase**: how far the mode has been shifted along the time axis. \
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_81995054] ### Definition
%[text] The magnitude-phase form of the Fourier series has a formal definition as a sum of phase-shifted cosines:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $f(t) = \\frac{A\_0}{2} +  \\sum\_{n=1}^{\\infty} A\_n \\cos\\left( \\frac{ n \\pi } {L}  t - \\phi\_n \\right)${"altText":"f(t) = \frac{A\_0}{2} +  \sum\_{n=1}^{\infty} A\_n \cos\left( \frac{ n \pi } {L}  t - \phi\_n \right)"}
%[text] This definition is related to the sine-cosine definition through
%[text]{"align":"center"} $A\_n = \\sqrt{a\_n^2 + b\_n^2}${"altText":"A\_n = \sqrt{a\_n^2 + b\_n^2}"}     and    $\\phi\_n = \\text{atan2}\\left( b\_n,a\_n\\right)${"altText":"\phi\_n = \text{atan2}\left( b\_n,a\_n\right)"}
%[text] Note that phase angle is computed using the two-argument arctangent. In MATLAB, this is implemented in the function [`atan2`](https://www.mathworks.com/help/matlab/ref/double.atan2.html). 
%%
%[text]  ![interact](text:image:6ff8) **Activity.** In this activity, you will compare the two discussed frequency domain representations: the sine-cosine representation and the magnitude-phase representation.
%[text] **Task 1.** Run the Fourier Series: Magnitude and Phase app.
%[text]{"align":"center"} [![MagPhaseApp](text:image:339b)](<matlab: run MagnitudePhase.mlapp>)
%[text]{"align":"center"} [`MagnitudePhase.mlapp`](<matlab: run MagnitudePhase.mlapp>)
%[text] Notice that the first pane (on the left) shows the sine-cosine version of the frequency domain ($a\_n${"altText":"a\_n"} and $b\_n${"altText":"b\_n"}). The right plane uses magnitude ($A\_n${"altText":"A\_n"}) and phase ($\\phi\_n${"altText":"\phi\_n"}) instead.
